[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note on the C build: `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no build type, so the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m must be built with **no optimisation flags**. This matters for observable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour, not just speed — see finding 7.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Everything below was found by running the two programs against each other; each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m entry says what diverged, what caused it, and what changed in the Rust code.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. Reference counting hit zero and the node was freed, but the graph kept the pointer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `delete_node` calls `free(node)` when the count reaches zero and never clears
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `graph->nodes[i]`, so every later operation reads memory the allocator owns. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program's visible behaviour after a deletion is therefore decided by glibc's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m malloc, not by the program's own logic. The whole of `src/heap.rs` exists to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reproduce that. The rest of this file is the list of ways the first attempt got
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it wrong.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducer for the general shape: `1 A / 7 A / 4 A` — the city can no longer be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m found, because the allocator overwrote the start of its name.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. Chunk reuse order was a plain LIFO stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** After deleting 8 or more cities and adding fresh ones, the new
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cities appeared under the wrong graph slots.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 C0 .. 1 C18   (19 cities)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7 C11 7 C10 7 C0 7 C8 7 C7 7 C12 7 C3 7 C18 7 C6 7 C15 7 C9 7 C1 7 C14 7 C4 7 C5
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 N0 .. 1 N14
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C hands the chunks back in the order `3 12 7 8 0 10 11 1 9 14 15 4 5 6 18`; a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m LIFO stack predicts `3 12 7 8 0 10 11 1 4 5 6 9 14 15 18`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Only the first 7 frees go on the per-thread cache. The rest go to the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unsorted bin, coalesce with their free neighbours, and are then re-split by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `_int_malloc` in a specific order.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/heap.rs` now models the parts of `_int_malloc`/`_int_free` that a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program allocating a single size class can observe: the tcache (LIFO, 7 entries),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the unsorted bin (freed at the head, scanned from the tail), the small bins
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (FIFO) and large bins (descending size, same-size chunks inserted in second
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m position), backward and forward consolidation, absorption into the top chunk,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `av->last_remainder`, and the "stash spares in the tcache" paths.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. Freed chunks were always treated as having lost their name
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** C printed the real city names for several deleted cities:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m City: O48 (ref_count: 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m while the translation printed an empty name.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The allocator only writes its list pointers over the *start of a free
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m block*. A chunk that is consolidated into a neighbour, or absorbed into the top
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m chunk, keeps its bytes untouched — including the name, which then still answers
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to `strcmp`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducer: 8 cities, delete 7 (filling the tcache), then delete the 8th. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8th borders the top chunk, is merged into it, and `4 C7` still finds it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Clobbering is applied per write site (`Arena::clobber`) instead of on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m every `free`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. Freeing an already free chunk did not abort
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** C printed a heap diagnostic on stderr and died of `SIGABRT`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (status 134 through a shell); the translation exited 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 A / 1 B / 2 A B 7 / 7 B / 6 A / 8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m       -> free(): double free detected in tcache 2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A stale edge lets `shallow_copy` raise the freed chunk's reference count back to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one, and `free_graph` then frees it a second time.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Not modelled at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `Arena::free` checks where the chunk currently sits and calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cio::malloc_printerr`, which writes the diagnostic to the unbuffered stderr and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `std::process::abort`s. Which diagnostic depends on the chunk's state:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | chunk state when freed again | glibc message |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | on the tcache | `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | in an unsorted/small/large bin | `double free or corruption (!prev)` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | *is* the top chunk | `double free or corruption (top)` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | in the interior of the merged top chunk | `corrupted size vs. prev_size while consolidating` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 5. stdout was flushed even when the program aborted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** After the abort in finding 4, C's stdout was *empty* (or truncated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to a whole number of 4096 byte blocks) while the translation printed everything.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** stdout is block buffered when it is not a terminal, and `abort()` does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not flush stdio. Whatever had not yet filled a 4096 byte block is lost.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `cio::Out` already buffered 4096 bytes at a time; `malloc_printerr` uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `std::process::abort`, which runs no destructors, so the buffer is dropped rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than flushed. `tests/differential.rs::abort_truncates_stdout_at_a_block_boundary`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pins this down: it asserts the C program's stdout is non-empty and a multiple of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4096 before comparing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 6. `shallow_copy` could revive a chunk sitting in a bin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Same shape as finding 4, but with more than 7 deletions pending so the chunk has
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fallen out of the tcache. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/differential.rs::double_free_or_corruption_prev`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 7. Not a translation bug: the C build type changes the C program's behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Building `c_src` with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` makes it abort on inputs where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the default (unoptimised) build exits 0, for example `1 A / 7 A / 8`. At `-O3`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m GCC drops the `node->ref_count--` store in `delete_node` because `free(node)` ends
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the object's lifetime, so the freed chunk keeps `ref_count == 1`; `free_graph`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then decrements it to 0 and frees it again. At `-O0` the store happens, the count
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m goes to `-1`, and nothing is freed twice.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` specifies no build type, so **the unoptimised build is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reference** and that is what the tests build and compare against.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Known limitations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are inputs where the C program's own behaviour cannot be reproduced. They
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are listed with reproducers so the next reader can check them rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rediscover them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### L1. Printing a freed node's name is not reproducible in C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 A / 7 A / 3 / 8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `print_graph` follows the dangling pointer and prints whatever sits where the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m name was. For a chunk on the tcache that is `tcache_entry::next`, which glibc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stores as `PROTECT_PTR(&e->next, NULL) == chunk_address >> 12`. The heap address
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m moves with ASLR, so the C program prints different bytes on every run:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ for i in 1 2 3; do ./c_src/build/driver < case | sed -n 38p; done
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m City: <d7><ac><02> (ref_count: 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m City: <8d><c7><01> (ref_count: 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m City: <f6>x<01> (ref_count: 0)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No translation can be byte identical to a reference that is not byte identical to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m itself. The Rust program prints an empty name there. Everything else about these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs *is* reproducible and does match:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stderr and the exit status;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the reference count read back out of the freed chunk (`ref_count: 0`);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the freed node's edges;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the surrounding output, byte for byte, before and after the name field.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/randomized.rs::freed_name_reads_are_not_reproducible_in_c` asserts exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that: 24 runs of the C program disagree with each other, and the Rust output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matches every one of them apart from 1..8 bytes in the name field. The randomized
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sweeps detect this class per input by running the C program twice, and for those
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs still require stderr and the exit status to match exactly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Measured rate: across ~2400 generated inputs, 711 fell into this class and all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 711 agreed on stderr and exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### L2. `find_shortest_path`'s own `malloc` is not modelled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `find_shortest_path` allocates `node_t *[count]` and frees it immediately. That
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m allocation is served from the same heap as the nodes, so it can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * carve bytes off the top chunk and overwrite a node chunk that was absorbed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   into it, or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * split a binned node-sized block, taking it out of the reuse order and leaving
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a 224 byte remainder that can never hold a node again.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducer for the first:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1 C0 .. 1 C7 / 7 C0 .. 7 C7 / 1 SRC / 2 SRC C7 3 / 4 C7 / 5 SRC C7 / 4 C7
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C reports `City 'C7' not found` on the second lookup (the path array overwrote
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the name); the translation still finds it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Modelling this needs a byte-granular allocator over several size classes rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than the single-size-class model in `heap.rs`, and the immediate output it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produces is a raw heap pointer, i.e. ASLR dependent (L1) in any case. Reaching it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m requires at least 8 deletions, a node absorbed into the top chunk, a successful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m path query and then a further operation on that same city.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Measured rate: 0 mismatches in ~2400 inputs from the general sweeps; 4 in 1196
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparable inputs (0.3%) from a sweep written specifically to provoke it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    210[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    211[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### L3. Which glibc diagnostic fires can depend on garbage heap metadata
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    212[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    213[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Once a chunk in the interior of the merged top chunk is freed, glibc reads a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    214[0m:[38;5;10m[49m header that is whatever the program last wrote there. The table in finding 4
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    215[0m:[38;5;10m[49m covers the four cases observed, but other inputs in that corner produce
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    216[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `double free or corruption (out)`, and one produced a `SIGSEGV` (status 139) from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    217[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dereferencing a wild edge pointer instead of any diagnostic at all. Reproducers
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    218[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are the two `other` and one `segv` inputs from the L2 sweep; they all require the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    219[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same depth of use-after-free as L2.
