[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust crate compiled from the start, but its behaviour diverged from the C on every input that touches a deleted city. [38;5;10mdelete_node[0m calls [38;5;10mfree()[0m and leaves the dangling pointer in the graph, so after any deletion the program's output is decided by glibc's malloc rather than by the program's own logic. Five real bugs, all now fixed in [38;5;10msrc/heap.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **Chunk reuse order** was a plain LIFO stack. Real glibc caches only the first 7 frees; the rest go through the unsorted bin, coalesce with free neighbours, and get re-split in a specific order. [38;5;10mheap.rs[0m now models the tcache, unsorted bin, small/large bins, backward and forward consolidation, top-chunk absorption and [38;5;10mlast_remainder[0m.[0m[0m
- **Freed names were always blanked.** The allocator only overwrites the [3mstart of a free block[23m, so a chunk consolidated into a neighbour or absorbed into the top chunk keeps its name and still answers to [38;5;10mstrcmp[0m — C finds a "deleted" city again.[0m[0m
- **Double frees didn't abort.** A stale edge lets [38;5;10mshallow_copy[0m revive a freed chunk's refcount; [38;5;10mfree_graph[0m then frees it twice. C prints a heap diagnostic and dies of [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m (four different messages depending on where the chunk sits).[0m[0m
- **stdout survived the abort.** [38;5;10mabort()[0m doesn't flush stdio, so C loses everything that hadn't filled a 4096-byte block. The Rust side now discards its buffer the same way.[0m[0m
- **Build flags matter.** Building [38;5;10mc_src[0m with [38;5;10m-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release[0m changes the C program's [3mbehaviour[23m (at [38;5;10m-O3[0m GCC drops the [38;5;10mref_count--[0m store before [38;5;10mfree[0m, causing aborts the unoptimised build doesn't have). [38;5;10mCMakeLists.txt[0m sets no build type, so the unoptimised build is the reference.[0m[0m
